window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.7","title":"The Paired t-Test","goal":"Test whether a drug lowers blood pressure in the lesson's 8 patients measured before and after, with a paired t-test.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the paired data","text":"Each patient appears in both lists, in the same order, so each 'after' belongs to a particular 'before'. The difference for each patient is their own change, and the lesson says every single one improved.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nbefore = np.array([142, 138, 155, 129, 147, 161, 133, 145])\nafter = np.array([135, 134, 148, 126, 139, 152, 130, 137])\ndiff = before - after\n\nprint('differences:', diff)\nprint(f'mean difference = {diff.mean():.3f}')\nprint(f'sd of differences = {diff.std(ddof=1):.3f}')","out":"differences: [7 4 7 3 8 9 3 8]\nmean difference = 6.125\nsd of differences = 2.416","figs":[]},{"title":"Run the paired t-test","text":"ttest_rel compares before and after pair by pair. A positive t means the 'before' values were higher. We also add a 95% confidence interval for the average drop.","code":"paired = stats.ttest_rel(before, after)\nci = paired.confidence_interval(confidence_level=0.95)\n\nprint(f't  = {paired.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'df = {paired.df}')\nprint(f'p  = {paired.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint(f'95% CI for the drop: ({ci.low:.2f}, {ci.high:.2f})')","out":"t  = 7.169\ndf = 7\np  = 0.0002\n95% CI for the drop: (4.10, 8.15)","figs":[]},{"title":"Same result as a one-sample test","text":"A paired t-test is just a one-sample t-test on the differences, testing whether their mean is 0. The numbers come out exactly the same.","code":"one_sample = stats.ttest_1samp(diff, popmean=0)\n\nprint(f't  = {one_sample.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'p  = {one_sample.pvalue:.4f}')\nprint('same t as the paired test?', np.isclose(one_sample.statistic, paired.statistic))","out":"t  = 7.169\np  = 0.0002\nsame t as the paired test? True","figs":[]},{"title":"What the wrong test says","text":"If we ignore the pairing and treat the 16 numbers as two independent groups, the big differences between patients swamp the 6 mmHg drop. The result is far less convincing.","code":"wrong = stats.ttest_ind(before, after, equal_var=False)\n\nprint(f'independent test: t = {wrong.statistic:.2f}, p = {wrong.pvalue:.3f}')\nprint(f'paired test:      t = {paired.statistic:.2f}, p = {paired.pvalue:.4f}')","out":"independent test: t = 1.25, p = 0.231\npaired test:      t = 7.17, p = 0.0002","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"before <- c(142, 138, 155, 129, 147, 161, 133, 145)\nafter  <- c(135, 134, 148, 126, 139, 152, 130, 137)\n\nt.test(before, after, paired = TRUE)\nt.test(before - after, mu = 0)    # same thing: one-sample test on the differences","expect":"t = 7.1692, df = 7, p-value = 0.0001823; 95 percent confidence interval: 4.105 to 8.145; mean difference 6.125","note":"paired = TRUE keeps the two vectors in matching order. Both calls give the same t, df, p-value and confidence interval."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Compare Means > Paired-Samples T Test","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / before after.\nBEGIN DATA\n142 135 138 134 155 148 129 126\n147 139 161 152 133 130 145 137\nEND DATA.\nT-TEST PAIRS=before WITH after (PAIRED)\n  /CRITERIA=CI(.95).","note":"In the Paired Samples Test table, the mean is before minus after (6.125). Read t, df and Sig. (2-tailed) from the same row."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput before after\n142 135\n138 134\n155 148\n129 126\n147 139\n161 152\n133 130\n145 137\nend\nttest before == after","note":"With two variables joined by ==, ttest does a paired test by default. Read the difference row and the two-sided Pr(|T| > |t|) value."},"try":["Change the last 'after' value from 137 to 145 and predict what happens to t and p, then run it.","Shuffle the order of the 'after' list (break the pairing) and run the paired test again. What happens?"]});
